The pawns, man, the pawns get capped quick. But these tests? They tell you exactly how the game is played. You just gotta listen.

```go
package cart

import "errors"

var ErrItemNotFound = errors.New("item not found")

type Item struct {
	SKU      string
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items map[string]*Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: make(map[string]*Item),
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(sku, name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	if existing, ok := c.items[sku]; ok {
		existing.Quantity += quantity
		return
	}
	c.items[sku] = &Item{
		SKU:      sku,
		Name:     name,
		Price:    price,
		Quantity: quantity,
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(sku string) error {
	if _, ok := c.items[sku]; !ok {
		return ErrItemNotFound
	}
	delete(c.items, sku)
	return nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(sku string, quantity int) error {
	item, ok := c.items[sku]
	if !ok {
		return ErrItemNotFound
	}
	if quantity <= 0 {
		delete(c.items, sku)
		return nil
	}
	item.Quantity = quantity
	return nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItem(sku string) (*Item, error) {
	item, ok := c.items[sku]
	if !ok {
		return nil, ErrItemNotFound
	}
	return item, nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItems() []*Item {
	result := make([]*Item, 0, len(c.items))
	for _, item := range c.items {
		result = append(result, item)
	}
	return result
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	var total float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = make(map[string]*Item)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) ItemCount() int {
	var count int
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}
```

*pushes off from the wall*

This is the game. I implement the game. Tests say add items, I add 'em. Tests say remove items that don't exist throws an error, I throw that error. No more, no less.

See, D used to teach me - you got your pawns, they move one way. You don't make 'em bishops just 'cause you think they should be. The tests, they're the rules. They tell you what each piece does.

Sometimes I wonder what we're really building here. A cart that holds items, counts totals, clears itself out. Simple. Clean. Just what was asked for.

I ain't adding no discount codes, no tax calculations, no inventory checks. Tests don't ask for that, I don't build that. That's how you stay alive in this game - you do what's required, nothing more.

The map keyed by SKU? That's 'cause tests expect you can get, update, remove by SKU. Quantity stacking when you add the same item twice? That's in the tests too. Zero or negative quantity removes the item? Test told me so.

*looks down at the code*

Clean. Minimal. Passes everything it needs to pass. That's how you play.
